<p><strong>Buying meat online</strong> can be a convenient way to reach producers and butchers that are not close to home, but the decision should not be based only on an attractive photograph and a price per kilo. With fresh food, the seller, cut, actual weight, packaging, delivery cost and arrival conditions all matter.</p>
<p>El Mercado de Origen brings together products from different sellers, with orders prepared from origin. This guide explains what to check before paying, what to look at when the parcel arrives and how to store fresh meat afterwards so that different offers can be compared properly.</p>

<h2>1. Check who is selling the meat</h2>
<p>A food shop should clearly identify the seller or producer behind the product. Knowing who prepares the order improves traceability and lets you check delivery, storage and customer-service conditions. On a marketplace, products from different sellers may have different preparation times, minimum orders and shipping charges.</p>
<p>Do not assume that every product on the site uses identical logistics. Read the individual product page and seller conditions before completing the basket.</p>

<h2>2. Read the cut, weight and format</h2>
<p>Terms such as “premium meat” mean very little without useful detail. A good listing should tell you what you are buying: type of meat, cut or preparation, weight, number of units where relevant and important storage information.</p>
<p>It also matters whether weight is fixed or approximate. Natural cuts vary in size, affecting both price and portions. When buying for a group, calculate using overall weight rather than only the number of trays.</p>
<p>For example, our <a href="/producto/lote-filetes-magro/">beef steak and lean-meat pack</a> states its overall contents and weight, making the quantity easier to understand.</p>

<h2>3. Compare the delivered price</h2>
<p>Price per kilo is useful, but it does not always show the real cost. Compare the product price after delivery fees and minimum-order requirements are included. A cheaper kilo can become more expensive when refrigerated shipping is added; another seller may include some or all delivery cost in the displayed price.</p>
<p>Check four things: <strong>total price, net weight, delivery charge and minimum order</strong>. That is the best way to compare equivalent purchases.</p>

<h2>4. Review fresh-delivery conditions</h2>
<p>The product page or shop conditions should explain how food requiring refrigeration is prepared and delivered. The aim is for the meat to travel under conditions consistent with its storage instructions while the primary packaging remains protected from damage and contamination.</p>
<p>There is no single correct packaging format. Vacuum packs, sealed trays and other appropriate systems can all work. The important point is that the product received matches the description and the pack is intact.</p>

<h2>5. What does vacuum packaging do?</h2>
<p>Vacuum packing reduces oxygen exposure and is common for fresh meat intended for transport and storage. Beef can look darker inside a low-oxygen pack and change colour again after opening as air reaches the surface.</p>
<p>That normal colour behaviour does not mean every damaged package should be ignored. If a pack is torn, leaking, open or otherwise inconsistent with the seller's promise, photograph it and contact the shop before consuming the meat.</p>

<h2>6. What to do when the order arrives</h2>
<p>Do not leave a fresh-meat box in the hallway for hours. Open it, check the items and package integrity, then move the products into refrigeration or freezing as appropriate. EFSA recommends keeping perishable food below 5°C and separating raw meat from ready-to-eat foods.</p>
<p>Organise products by use-by date as well. Use shorter-dated items first and freeze suitable products in good time if you will not cook them within the stated period. See our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-store-fresh-meat-in-fridge-when-to-freeze/">storing fresh meat in the fridge and deciding when to freeze it</a>.</p>

<h2>7. The use-by date matters more than smell</h2>
<p>For highly perishable food, a use-by date is about safety. EFSA advises that foods such as fresh meat should not be eaten after that date simply because they still look or smell normal. Harmful microorganisms cannot always be detected by the senses.</p>
<p>Plan storage before ordering a large quantity. Make room in the fridge and freezer before the delivery arrives.</p>

<h2>8. Fresh and frozen are different purchases</h2>
<p>Both formats can be useful, but you should know which one you ordered. Meat sold as fresh should have descriptions and delivery conditions consistent with refrigerated storage. Frozen meat should remain frozen and then be stored according to its label.</p>
<p>Fresh meat is convenient when you plan to cook soon. Frozen meat supports longer planned storage. Neither is automatically superior; clear information before purchase is what matters.</p>

<h2>9. Read reviews in context</h2>
<p>An average rating may combine flavour, delivery speed, customer service and packaging. Look for comments about the exact product: condition on arrival, cut, whether weight matched the description and how it was packed.</p>
<p>Reviews do not replace legal information or the product description, but they can show whether the buying experience is consistent.</p>

<h2>10. What if the parcel is damaged or seems too warm?</h2>
<p>Keep the labels, take photographs and contact the seller promptly. If there is a reasonable concern about the cold chain or package integrity, do not taste the meat to “see if it is okay”. Smell and flavour cannot reveal every microbiological hazard.</p>
<p>The seller should be able to explain the next step and what evidence is needed to review the delivery issue.</p>

<h2>11. Mixed packs can spread delivery cost</h2>
<p>When transport is a significant part of the order, a planned meat pack may be more efficient than one small tray. It only makes sense, however, when you will use or correctly freeze all the contents. Wasting extra meat is not a saving.</p>
<p>This is particularly useful for families and gatherings. For grill planning, see our guide to <a href="/en/what-meat-to-buy-for-a-barbecue-how-much-per-person/">what meat to buy for a barbecue and how much per person</a>.</p>

<h2>12. Signs of a well-presented online meat shop</h2>
<ul>
<li>It clearly identifies the seller or producer.</li>
<li>It describes cut, weight, format and unit count.</li>
<li>Delivery conditions and charges are visible before payment.</li>
<li>Fresh-food storage information is provided.</li>
<li>There is a clear customer-service channel.</li>
<li>Reviews or sufficient seller information can be checked.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which meat makes most sense to buy online?</h2>
<p>Online buying is particularly useful when it gives you access to a producer, cut, quality level or value that is difficult to find nearby. It also works well for planned family orders, barbecues and mixed packs because logistics are spread across more product.</p>
<p>If you need one steak for dinner tonight, a nearby physical shop may be more practical. The main advantage of online meat shopping is wider choice and more direct access, not replacing every local purchase.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is buying meat online safe?</h3>
<p>It can be when the seller uses appropriate hygiene, packaging, transport and cold-chain procedures. As the customer, read the available information and refrigerate the order promptly on arrival.</p>
<h3>Does meat have to arrive frozen?</h3>
<p>No. Fresh meat should arrive under the refrigerated conditions stated by the seller. Frozen meat should remain frozen. What matters is that the product arrives in the format advertised.</p>
<h3>Why can vacuum-packed beef look darker?</h3>
<p>Low oxygen changes the state of beef pigments. After the pack is opened, exposure to air can make the colour become brighter red.</p>
<h3>What if I will not cook it soon?</h3>
<p>Read the label. If the product is suitable for freezing and you will not use it within its refrigerated period, freeze it before reaching the use-by date.</p>

<h2>Buy fresh meat online from El Mercado de Origen</h2>
<p>Compare seller, cut, format, weight and final delivered cost. The aim is not to buy more, but to know exactly what you are receiving and how to store it correctly from the moment it arrives.</p>
